#v1.2.0 - [10/03/2026]
TCP server:
- Added optional `onPoll()` hook: a derived server that declares a public `void onPoll()` gets it called once per loop iteration on the server thread, so it can call `send_data()`/`disconnect_client()` for traffic not triggered by a client (detected at compile time, zero cost when absent)
- Added `send_data(client_id, const uint8_t* data, size_t size)`; the `std::vector`/`std::string` overloads forward to it without copying

Multicast:
- Added `MulticastSender::send_data(const uint8_t* data, size_t size)`; the `std::vector`/`std::string` overloads forward to it without copying
- The group address is resolved once in `start()` instead of on every send; an invalid address is logged there and every send then fails and counts a send error

#v1.1.0 - [10/02/2026]
Breaking changes:
- Replaced the unused `TCPServerConfig::connection_timeout` with `idle_timeout` (default 0 = disabled), which disconnects clients with no traffic in either direction
- Deleted the move constructor and move assignment of `TCPServerBase`, `TCPClientBase`, `MulticastSender` and `MulticastReceiverBase`
- TCP server and client no longer install a process-wide `SIGPIPE` handler; writes to a closed peer fail via `MSG_NOSIGNAL` / `SO_NOSIGPIPE` instead
- CMake no longer forces `CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release` or adds global `DEBUG`/`NDEBUG` definitions

TCP server:
- `send_data()` never blocks the server thread: data a slow client cannot take is queued per client and flushed when the socket becomes writable
- Added `TCPServerConfig::max_pending_send_bytes` (default 16 MiB) to cap each client's send queue; a message that would exceed it is rejected whole
- `max_connections` is now enforced (`<= 0` = unlimited); connections beyond it are closed immediately
- `port = 0` binds a free port; added `port()` to read the bound port
- `onClientDisconnected()` fires exactly once for every client that leaves, including `disconnect_client()`, failed sends and idle timeouts, and never from inside another callback
- `stop()` may be called from server callbacks, and a stopped server can be restarted
- `get_connected_client_count()` is safe to call from any thread

TCP client:
- `server_address` accepts hostnames as well as IPv4 literals
- `send_data()` is lock-free and safe from any number of threads, including concurrently with `disconnect()`; it waits for socket room without spinning
- `onConnected()` runs on the `connect()` thread before the client thread starts; calling `disconnect()` from it makes `connect()` return `false`
- The client thread blocks in `poll()` when idle instead of busy-looping; it busy-polls only when `cpu_affinity` is set
- A disconnected client can reconnect by calling `connect()` again

Multicast:
- `MulticastSender::send_data()` is lock-free and safe concurrently with `stop()`, and returns within about 1 ms of `stop()` when the send buffer is full
- `MulticastReceiverBase::stop()` may be called from `handle_multicast_data()`

General:
- Fixed TCP client/server shutdown races, partial reads and connection-count races
- Destroying a server, client or receiver that is still running logs an error and invokes the overridable `SLICK_SOCKET_ON_UNSAFE_DESTROY()` hook (asserts in debug builds)
- Default no-op `LOG_*` macros reference their arguments, so log-only variables do not trigger unused warnings
- Headers compile cleanly at `/W4` and `-Wall -Wextra -Wpedantic`; added `SLICK_SOCKET_WARNINGS_AS_ERRORS` option for tests and examples
- `slick::socket` propagates C++20 to consumers via `cxx_std_20`
- Release archives contain the `cmake --install` tree with CMake package files; the Windows archive includes `wepoll.h` and Debug (`wepoll_libd`) and Release (`wepoll_lib`) libraries
- Added real-network integration tests

#v1.0.6 - [02/06/2026]
- Removed shared build. There is no exports
- Converted to truly header-only library (INTERFACE target on all platforms)
- Added wepoll dependency via vcpkg on Windows with FetchContent fallback
- Simplified CMake configuration to use single INTERFACE library
- Automatic wepoll fetch and build if not found

#v1.0.5 - [02/04/2026]
- Add CMake package configuration support for find_package()
- Add cmake/slick-socketConfig.cmake.in template
- Add slick-socketConfigVersion.cmake generation with SameMajorVersion compatibility
- Install CMake config files to lib/cmake/slick-socket/
- Export library targets with proper install commands
- Enable downstream projects to use find_package(slick-socket)
- Enable modern CMake CRT handling. Only sets a default if the user/toolchain hasn't already specified it

#v1.0.4 - [01/12/2026]
- Rename repository from slick_socket to slick-socket (hyphenated naming follows recommended convention)
- Changed export name from slick::slick_socket to slick::socket
- Refactor repository name in CMake configuration files
- Update documentation and build references to use new repository name

#v1.0.3 - [12/09/2025]
- Add cmake options to turn ON/OFF static/shared build

#v1.0.2 - [12/05/2025]
- Change MulticastSender from template class to regular class (breaking change)
- Upgrade Google Test to v1.17.0
- Fix MSVC MSB8028 warnings about intermediate directory conflicts
- Set explicit RUNTIME_OUTPUT_DIRECTORY for example executables
- Add MSVC runtime library configuration
- Remove redundant VS_GLOBAL_IntDir properties from custom targets

#v1.0.1 - [10/21/2025]
- Change include folder structure from include/slick_socket/ to include/slick/
- Change namespace from slick_socket to slick::socket
- Add GitHub CI workflow
- Fix MacOS build
- Fix example log macros doesn't work without format args
- Added socket binding (lines 165-176) - Bind to a local address before setting multicast options. This is required on some platforms like macOS for multicast senders.
- Fixed setsockopt calls (lines 180, 189, 203) - Changed from passing raw pointer to using reinterpret_cast<const char*>(&value). While this is technically the same thing, it's more explicit about the expected type
- Fix binding multiple sockts failed on MacOS
- Skip multicast sending tests on GitHub CI, muticast sending is not supported.

#1.0.0 - [09/26/2025]
- Initial Release